<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Torvioll</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1444">1444</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Torvioll was fought on 29 June 1444 in the plain of Torvioll in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg</a>, leading the forces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-lezhe">League of Lezhë</a>, defeated an Ottoman army under Ali Pasha. Skanderbeg hid part of his troops in the woods and attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> from the flanks and rear. It was his first major victory over the Ottomans.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-torvioll.jpg" alt="Battle of Torvioll" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NY onja Christian · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Torv.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg</a>, son of the prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjon-kastrioti">Gjon Kastrioti</a>, had served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sipahi">sipahi</a>, a cavalry commander. After the Ottoman defeat at the Battle of Niš he deserted with 300 other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> and, by forging a letter from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a> to the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kruje">Krujë</a>, became lord of the city in November 1443. On 2 March 1444 he gathered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>'s nobles in the Venetian-held town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lezhe">Alessio</a> (Lezhë), among them George Arianiti, Pal Dukagjini, Andrea Thopia and Lekë Zaharia, who formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-lezhe">League of Lezhë</a> and chose him as its captain.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a>, seeking to end the movement at once, sent Ali Pasha, one of his most favoured commanders, from Üsküp (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a>) in June 1444 with 25,000 to 40,000 troops along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drin">Drin</a> towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kruje">Krujë</a>. Skanderbeg raised 10,000 to 15,000 men from the League, 8,000 of them on horseback; most were feudal levies, but over 2,000 were paid permanent troops.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg</a> chose the battlefield himself, a plain in Lower Dibra north of Peshkopi, thought to be the Plain of Shumbat: 11.2 kilometres long and 4.9 kilometres wide, surrounded by hills and forests. He placed 3,000 men under five commanders, among them Hamza Kastrioti, in the woods with orders to attack the Ottoman wings and rear only on a signal. On the night before the battle the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> rejoiced in advance, certain of victory, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> put out their campfires and rested, ignoring Ottoman parties that came to provoke them.</p><p>On the morning of 29 June the Albanian army, divided into three parts, feigned a retreat and dispersed into the mountains and forests, drawing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> into the gorge of Torvioll. As Ali Pasha prepared to declare his victory, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> regrouped and attacked, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg</a>'s signal Hamza's hidden horsemen surrounded and defeated the Ottoman forces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Between 7,000 and 10,000 Ottoman troops died and around 500 to 2,000 were taken prisoner, while 3,800 to 4,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> were killed or wounded. The victory was praised in the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, raised the morale of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg</a>'s followers and strengthened his position as leader of the League. It also encouraged Pope Eugenius IV and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi">John Hunyadi</a> to organise a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in the autumn of 1444.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Torvioll” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The league was a military alliance rather than a unified state: each lord kept his own lands, while Skanderbeg led a mobile army that used mountain terrain to defeat larger Ottoman forces, as at <b>Torvioll</b> in 1444 and Albulena in 1457.</blockquote><a href="/state/league-of-lezhe">League of Lezhë</a></li></ul></section>
